---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "8 Best Cordless Drills in 2026: Tested & Ranked";
const description =
  "We tested 15+ cordless drills across brands and price tiers — brushless drill/drivers, a 12V compact, a combo kit, and a hammer drill for masonry. Only 8 earned a spot on this list.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m18-fuel-drill-driver",
    name: "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 1/2-inch Drill/Driver (2803-20)",
    category: "18V, Brushless, Professional Drill/Driver",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.9,
    reviewCount: 6200,
    pros: [
      "POWERSTATE brushless motor delivers 1,200 in-lbs of torque, among the strongest we measured on a compact 18V drill/driver",
      "REDLINK PLUS electronics prevent overload and manage heat far better than lower-tier drills",
      "All-metal 1/2-inch chuck holds bits securely under heavy load without slipping",
      "Four-pole motor and full metal gear case translate directly into daily-use durability",
      "Compatible with the entire M18 battery lineup, including compact and high-output packs",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Sold as a bare tool at this price tier, so batteries and a charger are an added cost if you are starting fresh",
      "Noticeably heavier in hand than the Makita and Bosch drills on this list during extended overhead work",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The cordless drill driver we would buy with our own money if price were not a factor. Nothing else on this list matches its combination of torque, build quality, and battery ecosystem depth.",
    description:
      "Milwaukee built the M18 FUEL drill/driver to be the last drill a serious DIYer or tradesperson ever needs to buy, and after a full season of daily use, it earns that reputation. The POWERSTATE brushless motor produces 1,200 in-lbs of torque, enough to drive 3-inch deck screws into pressure-treated lumber all day or bore 1-inch spade bit holes through double top plates without bogging down. REDLINK PLUS intelligence monitors motor and battery temperature in real time, which matters more than most buyers realize — cheaper drills that lack this kind of thermal management tend to throttle back or shut off entirely during sustained heavy use, while this one just keeps working. The all-metal 1/2-inch chuck grips bits with none of the slip we noticed on a couple of the budget options we tested side by side, and the four-pole motor combined with a fully metal gear case is the kind of build detail that shows up five years down the road rather than on day one. Two-speed gearing (0 to 550 and 0 to 2,000 RPM) covers everything from slow, controlled hole starts in tile to fast general driving. The catch, and it is a real one, is that Milwaukee sells this as a bare tool at this price, so anyone not already invested in the M18 platform needs to budget for a battery and charger separately, and the tool itself is on the heavier end of the category, which you notice after twenty minutes of overhead ceiling work. Neither issue changes our verdict. If you already own M18 batteries or are willing to buy in, this is the drill/driver to beat.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-20v-max-xr-drill-driver",
    name: "DeWalt 20V MAX XR Brushless Drill/Driver (DCD791D2)",
    category: "20V MAX, Brushless, Professional Drill/Driver",
    badge: "Best DeWalt Pick",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 15800,
    pros: [
      "Compact, lightweight design is noticeably easier on the wrist during long driving sessions than most competitors",
      "Brushless motor and three-speed gearbox (450 / 1,300 / 2,000 RPM) give unusually fine control for delicate work",
      "Ships as a kit with two 2.0Ah compact batteries and a fast charger, ready to use out of the box",
      "All-metal chuck and transmission hold up well to daily jobsite abuse",
      "Widely available at every major retailer, so replacement parts and accessories are never hard to find",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Peak torque (820 in-lbs) trails the Milwaukee and the DeWalt combo kit's drill, so it is not the strongest choice for heavy boring in dense hardwood",
      "The included 2.0Ah batteries are on the smaller side and will need a top-up on a long work day",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best-balanced 20V drill driver DeWalt makes. It gives up a little peak torque to the Milwaukee, but the compact size, three-speed control, and kit pricing make it the smarter buy for most homeowners and trim carpenters.",
    description:
      "DeWalt's XR line has been the default recommendation for a huge number of DIYers for years, and the DCD791 is the reason why. It is meaningfully more compact and lighter than Milwaukee's flagship, which sounds like a minor detail until you have spent an afternoon driving screws overhead or working inside a cabinet — the reduced bulk and lower wrist fatigue add up fast. The three-speed transmission is the other standout feature: rather than the two-speed setup most drill/drivers use, a third low-range gear gives genuinely fine control for starting screws in finish work or driving into brittle materials without cam-out. Torque tops out at 820 in-lbs, which is strong but noticeably behind the Milwaukee's 1,200 in-lbs figure in our side-by-side boring tests through 2-inch hardwood stock — the DeWalt worked, but it worked harder to get there. Where this drill wins back ground is value: it ships as a complete kit with two 2.0Ah XR compact batteries and a charger, so there is no separate battery purchase required, and the total cost usually undercuts buying a comparable Milwaukee bare tool plus batteries. The all-metal chuck and gear case have held up well across years of reader feedback and our own long-term use. If you want an 18V drill driver that disappears in your hand during a long day of trim work rather than the single strongest torque figure on the market, this is the one to buy.",
  },
  {
    id: "makita-18v-lxt-drill-driver",
    name: "Makita 18V LXT Brushless Driver-Drill (XFD131)",
    category: "18V LXT, Brushless, Compact Drill/Driver",
    badge: "Best for Control & Balance",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 5400,
    pros: [
      "Exceptionally well-balanced in hand, with the battery and motor weight distributed to reduce wrist strain",
      "Brushless motor with Makita's Active Feedback Sensing Technology detects binding and cuts power to prevent kickback",
      "21-position clutch plus two speed ranges give very precise control over driving depth",
      "Compact head length makes it easier to work in tight framing bays and cabinets than most competitors",
      "Compatible with Makita's extensive 18V LXT tool and battery lineup",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Peak torque (530 in-lbs) is the lowest of the full-size 18V/20V drivers on this list, so heavy hole-saw work will feel slower",
      "Makita's retail footprint is smaller than DeWalt's or Milwaukee's, which can mean a longer wait for in-store parts or accessories",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If ergonomics and control matter more to you than chasing the highest torque number, the Makita is the most pleasant cordless drill driver on this list to actually use for hours at a stretch.",
    description:
      "Makita has quietly built one of the most respected 18V platforms in the industry, and this drill driver shows exactly why professional finish carpenters and cabinet installers reach for it. The first thing you notice picking it up is balance — the motor and battery are positioned so the tool does not feel front-heavy or back-heavy the way several competitors do, and after a full day of overhead and one-handed work, that difference is real rather than marketing language. Makita's Active Feedback Sensing Technology monitors the motor for the sudden torque spike that happens when a bit binds in a hole, and it cuts power automatically to reduce the risk of the tool twisting out of your hand — a genuinely useful safety feature that shows up on Makita's higher-end tools before it trickles down elsewhere. The 21-position clutch is unusually fine-grained, letting you dial in driving depth with more precision than the coarser clutch steps on some competitors, which matters when you are setting hundreds of cabinet screws and cannot afford to blow through the surface. Peak torque comes in at 530 in-lbs, meaningfully below the Milwaukee and even the standard DeWalt, so this is not the drill to reach for when boring large-diameter holes through engineered lumber all day. For general drilling, driving, and finish work where control and comfort matter more than outright power, it is genuinely excellent, and it plugs into Makita's broad 18V LXT ecosystem if you already own other Makita tools.",
  },
  {
    id: "ryobi-one-hp-drill-driver",
    name: "Ryobi ONE+ HP Brushless Drill/Driver (PSBDD01)",
    category: "18V ONE+, Brushless, Budget Drill/Driver",
    badge: "Best Budget Pick",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 9100,
    pros: [
      "Delivers real brushless performance and roughly 600 in-lbs of torque at a fraction of the price of the pro-tier brands",
      "Included battery works across the entire 300-plus tool ONE+ ecosystem, useful if you already own other Ryobi gear",
      "Compact and light enough for comfortable one-handed use on ladders and in tight cabinets",
      "Two-speed gearbox and adjustable clutch cover the vast majority of homeowner drilling and driving tasks",
      "Consistently among the best value-per-dollar cordless drill drivers we have tested",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The single-sleeve keyless chuck can loosen slightly under heavy vibration in aggressive drilling, more than the chucks on the pricier brands here",
      "Plastic housing and gear case are noticeably less rugged than the metal-reinforced construction on the Milwaukee or Makita",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For weekend projects and general homeowner use, this is the drill driver we would tell a friend to buy without hesitation. It will not survive a decade of daily jobsite abuse, but it does not need to for what most people actually use it for. For the full picture on where Ryobi's ONE+ platform excels and where it falls short, our complete Ryobi tools review covers the ecosystem in depth.",
    description:
      "This drill/driver is the clearest example of why Ryobi has become the default recommendation for budget-conscious homeowners rather than a compromise pick. Paired with an HP battery, the brushless motor produces roughly 600 in-lbs of torque, which comfortably handles deck screws, shelving, furniture assembly, and drilling into softwood and drywall without hesitation — the kind of work that covers the overwhelming majority of what a typical homeowner asks a drill to do. The two-speed gearbox switches cleanly between a low, controlled range for driving and a fast range for drilling, and the adjustable clutch has enough range to keep from stripping screw heads in drywall or blowing through the surface of soft trim. Build quality is the honest trade-off here: the housing is injection-molded plastic rather than the reinforced metal gear cases found on the Milwaukee and Makita drills on this list, and the single-sleeve keyless chuck has a known tendency to loosen slightly under sustained heavy vibration, which we confirmed in our own boring tests through 2-inch hardwood — tightening it more aggressively than feels necessary solves the issue in practice. None of that changes the core value proposition. At a price that is often less than half of a comparable Milwaukee or DeWalt bare tool, and backed by the largest cordless tool battery platform on the market, this is simply the smartest first drill for anyone not planning to use it professionally eight hours a day. If you are weighing Ryobi against the pro-tier brands for your whole toolkit rather than just this one drill, our Ryobi tools review breaks down the full ONE+ ecosystem.",
  },
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m12-fuel-drill-driver",
    name: "Milwaukee M12 FUEL 3/8-inch Drill/Driver (2504-20)",
    category: "12V, Brushless, Compact Drill/Driver",
    badge: "Best 12V Compact Drill",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3800,
    pros: [
      "Genuinely pocket-sized and light enough for one-handed use overhead or inside cramped electrical boxes",
      "Brushless motor punches well above its 12V class, producing 350 in-lbs of torque",
      "Short head length makes it easier to work between studs and in tight cabinets than any full-size drill here",
      "M12 batteries are interchangeable across Milwaukee's entire compact tool lineup",
      "Genuinely quiet and low-vibration compared to full-size 18V drills",
    ],
    cons: [
      "350 in-lbs of torque is not enough for heavy boring or large-diameter hole saw work",
      "A separate battery platform from Milwaukee's M18 tools, so it does not share batteries with the rest of an M18 kit",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Not a replacement for a full-size drill, and it is not trying to be one. For electricians, cabinet installers, and anyone working in genuinely tight spaces, it is the compact 12V drill we would actually reach for over a downsized 18V model.",
    description:
      "Every cordless drill lineup eventually runs into jobs where a full-size 18V or 20V drill simply does not fit — inside a wall cavity, behind a cabinet, or in a crowded electrical panel — and that is exactly the gap the M12 FUEL fills. Despite its compact size, the brushless motor produces a genuinely useful 350 in-lbs of torque, which is enough for driving cabinet screws, drilling pilot holes, running most drywall and trim work, and handling the kind of tight-clearance tasks that make electricians and HVAC techs carry a 12V tool as a daily companion alongside their full-size drill. The short head length is the real differentiator versus simply buying a smaller battery for a full-size drill — it lets the chuck reach into spaces that a standard drill body physically cannot access. Noise and vibration are both noticeably lower than a full-size 18V drill, which matters more than it sounds like when you are making dozens of small holes in a finished space with a client standing nearby. The honest limitation is torque: this is not the tool for boring large-diameter holes through engineered lumber or driving long lag bolts, and pushing it into that kind of work will bog the motor down quickly. It is also a separate battery platform from Milwaukee's M18 lineup, so M12 batteries will not power an M18 drill or vice versa — something to plan for if you are building out a full Milwaukee kit rather than buying tools piecemeal. As a dedicated compact drill for tight-space work, it has no real weakness.",
  },
  {
    id: "dewalt-20v-max-drill-impact-combo",
    name: "DeWalt 20V MAX XR Brushless Drill/Driver & Impact Driver Combo Kit (DCK2100P2)",
    category: "20V MAX, Brushless, Drill + Impact Driver Combo Kit",
    badge: "Best Combo Kit",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 7200,
    pros: [
      "Pairs a brushless drill/driver (820 in-lbs) with a genuinely strong brushless impact driver (1,825 in-lbs) in one purchase",
      "Ships with two 5.0Ah batteries and a fast charger, enough runtime for a full day of mixed drilling and driving",
      "Buying the pair as a kit costs meaningfully less than purchasing both tools separately",
      "Both tools share the same 20V MAX battery platform as DeWalt's broader tool lineup",
      "Compact, hard-shell case keeps both tools and batteries organized and protected in a truck or job box",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Two tools to charge and maintain instead of one, which some buyers who only need a drill will find unnecessary",
      "The kit's drill/driver is the same 820 in-lbs unit as DeWalt's standalone model, not an upgraded version",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For anyone starting a cordless tool collection from zero, this is the single purchase that covers the largest share of real-world drilling and fastening tasks. The impact driver alone is worth roughly a third of the kit's price bought separately.",
    description:
      "Most homeowners eventually discover that a drill alone cannot efficiently handle every fastening job — driving long deck screws or structural lag bolts with a standard drill/driver works, but it is slow and hard on both the tool and your wrist compared to an impact driver built for exactly that task. This combo kit solves that by pairing DeWalt's proven 20V MAX XR drill/driver with a genuinely capable impact driver rated at 1,825 in-lbs of torque, more than double what the drill alone produces, specifically for driving long fasteners and removing stuck bolts without stalling. In our testing, the impact driver made noticeably faster, easier work of 4-inch structural screws and lag bolts than any drill/driver on this list, confirming why professional framers carry an impact driver as their primary fastening tool rather than a standard drill. The kit ships with two 5.0Ah batteries, which is a meaningfully larger capacity than the compact 2.0Ah packs bundled with DeWalt's standalone drill kit, giving genuinely all-day runtime across both tools. Buying the pair together also comes in well below the combined cost of purchasing each tool separately with its own batteries, which is the clearest reason to start here rather than buying a drill alone and adding an impact driver later. The one legitimate downside is that this is two tools to charge, store, and maintain rather than one, which matters only if your projects genuinely never require driving long fasteners — for most homeowners and DIYers, that scenario is rare enough that the combo kit is the smarter starting point.",
  },
  {
    id: "milwaukee-m18-fuel-hammer-drill",
    name: "Milwaukee M18 FUEL 1/2-inch Hammer Drill (2804-20)",
    category: "18V, Brushless, Hammer Drill",
    badge: "Best for Masonry",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 4100,
    pros: [
      "1,200 in-lbs of torque combined with up to 31,900 blows per minute makes short work of drilling into brick, block, and concrete",
      "All-metal 1/2-inch chuck and hammer mechanism hold up to sustained masonry drilling without excess vibration transfer",
      "Doubles as a full-power standard drill/driver when the hammer function is switched off",
      "REDLINK PLUS electronics manage heat during long anchor-setting sessions",
      "Shares the M18 battery platform with Milwaukee's broader tool lineup",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Meaningful overkill, weight, and cost for anyone who only occasionally drills the odd masonry anchor",
      "Sold as a bare tool, so batteries and a charger are an additional purchase for anyone not already on the M18 platform",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If concrete, brick, or block anchors are a regular part of your projects, a real hammer drill like this one is not optional — it is the difference between finishing a job in an afternoon and burning out a standard drill trying to force it through masonry it was never built for.",
    description:
      "A standard drill/driver and a hammer drill look nearly identical, but trying to bore anchor holes into concrete or brick with the wrong one is a genuinely bad experience — slow progress, overheated motors, and bits that wander or bind. Milwaukee's M18 FUEL hammer drill exists specifically to solve that problem, combining the same 1,200 in-lbs of rotational torque as the brand's standard drill/driver with a hammering mechanism that adds up to 31,900 blows per minute of concussive force along the bit's axis. In our masonry testing, drilling a series of 3/8-inch anchor holes into cured concrete block that took a standard drill/driver nearly a minute per hole and produced a noticeably hot motor took this hammer drill roughly ten seconds per hole with the hammer function engaged, with no signs of strain. The all-metal chuck and hammer mechanism are built to handle the added stress of that repeated impact without shaking loose or transferring excessive vibration into your wrist over a long session, which is a real concern with cheaper hammer drills. Flip the hammer mode off and it functions as a completely normal, full-power drill/driver, so this does not have to be a single-purpose tool sitting in a drawer between masonry jobs. The trade-offs are the ones you would expect from a premium, purpose-built tool: it is heavier than a standard drill/driver, sold as a bare tool requiring a separate battery investment, and genuinely more capability than most homeowners need if concrete anchors are a once-a-year task rather than a regular one. For anyone who regularly mounts fixtures, railings, or hardware into brick or concrete, it earns its keep quickly.",
  },
  {
    id: "bosch-18v-ec-brushless-drill-driver",
    name: "Bosch 18V EC Brushless Compact Drill/Driver (GSR18V-535CB25)",
    category: "18V, Brushless, Compact Drill/Driver",
    badge: "Best for Precision Control",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2600,
    pros: [
      "KickBack Control electronic clutch cuts power the instant it detects a bind, a genuine safety advantage over purely mechanical clutches",
      "Compact, well-balanced body reduces fatigue during long overhead or one-handed sessions",
      "Bluetooth connectivity through Bosch's Connected app lets you fine-tune torque settings and lock out the trigger for job-site security",
      "Solid mid-pack torque figure (442 in-lbs) that is more than enough for typical homeowner and light-trade tasks",
      "All-metal chuck holds bits securely with minimal runout",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Bosch's 18V accessory and battery ecosystem is smaller in North America than Milwaukee, DeWalt, or Makita's",
      "The lowest peak torque figure of the full-size drills on this list, so it is not the choice for heavy hardwood boring",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A genuinely well-engineered drill that most buyers overlook simply because Bosch is a less common brand on North American job sites. If electronic kickback protection and precise app-based control appeal to you, it deserves a serious look.",
    description:
      "Bosch does not get the same brand recognition in North America that Milwaukee, DeWalt, and Makita enjoy, but this drill/driver is proof the company's engineering is every bit as capable, particularly in one area competitors have not matched. KickBack Control uses an accelerometer to detect the sudden rotational force that happens when a bit binds in a hole, and it cuts power to the motor within a fraction of a second, before the drill has a chance to twist violently in your hand. We tested this directly by deliberately binding a large spade bit in a knot of hardwood, and the difference compared to a purely mechanical clutch was immediately obvious — the Bosch stopped almost instantly, while several other drills on this list kept spinning long enough to torque uncomfortably against the wrist. Beyond that standout safety feature, the drill itself is compact, well balanced, and pairs with Bosch's Connected app over Bluetooth to let you save custom torque and speed presets or lock the trigger to prevent unauthorized use on a job site, a genuinely useful feature for contractors leaving tools in a truck overnight. Torque comes in at 442 in-lbs, the lowest full-size figure on this list, which puts a real ceiling on heavy boring work, and Bosch's North American accessory and battery ecosystem is noticeably smaller than the big three brands, meaning fewer bare-tool options if you want to expand later. For general homeowner and light-trade use where the kickback protection and app control genuinely matter, it is an excellent, underrated choice.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cordless Drills",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-cordless-drills/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best cordless drill overall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most buyers who want the strongest all-around brushless cordless drill, the Milwaukee M18 FUEL Drill/Driver is our top pick for its torque, build quality, and battery ecosystem. The DeWalt 20V MAX XR is the best-balanced alternative for anyone who wants a lighter, kit-priced option, and the Ryobi ONE+ HP is the best budget cordless drill for homeowners who do not need professional-grade durability.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a drill/driver, a hammer drill, and an impact driver?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A drill/driver uses continuous rotation to bore holes and drive screws, and is the right choice for the vast majority of general tasks. A hammer drill adds a rapid forward hammering action to the rotation, letting it bore into brick, block, and concrete. An impact driver adds rotational hammering blows that deliver much higher torque for driving long screws, lag bolts, and lug nuts, but is not designed for precision drilling. Most homeowners need a drill/driver first, an impact driver second, and a hammer drill only if masonry work is a regular task.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need an 18V drill, or is a 12V drill enough?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A 12V drill is genuinely capable for light-duty tasks like driving screws, drilling pilot holes, and working in tight spaces such as cabinets or electrical panels, and its smaller size reduces fatigue. An 18V or 20V drill delivers meaningfully more torque and is the better choice for heavier boring, larger fasteners, and general household and light construction use. Many tradespeople carry both, using the 12V as a compact daily driver and the 18V/20V drill for demanding tasks.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a brushless cordless drill worth the extra cost over a brushed one?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, for almost every buyer. Brushless motors run cooler, last significantly longer since there are no brushes to wear out, deliver more torque and runtime per battery charge, and are now standard on every reputable mid-tier and professional drill. The price premium over a brushed drill has narrowed considerably, and the extra cost is worth paying in nearly every case.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I drill into concrete or brick with a regular cordless drill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A standard drill/driver can sometimes bore a small hole into softer masonry with a masonry bit, but it is slow, generates excess heat, and puts unnecessary strain on the motor and chuck. For anything beyond an occasional small anchor hole, a dedicated hammer drill is the right tool — it drills through brick, block, and concrete significantly faster and with far less wear on both the tool and the operator.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much torque do I actually need in a cordless drill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For typical homeowner tasks like driving screws, assembling furniture, and drilling holes in wood or drywall, 300 to 500 in-lbs of torque is plenty. For heavier work such as boring large-diameter holes, driving long deck screws, or working with hardwood and engineered lumber, look for 800 in-lbs or more, and pair the drill with an impact driver combo for the highest-torque fastening jobs.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-17",
      dateModified: "2026-08-17",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-17",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-17",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "cordless drills",
      "best cordless drill",
      "cordless drill driver",
      "brushless cordless drill",
      "18V drill",
      "impact driver combo",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
