        <div id="tool-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Drill vs. Driver vs. Hammer Drill vs. Impact Driver
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most common point of confusion for anyone shopping for a cordless drill, and getting it wrong means buying a tool that fights you on the job it's supposed to make easy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Drill and Driver: Two Jobs, One Tool</h3>
          <p>
            In common usage, a drill bores holes and a driver drives screws — but nearly every consumer and professional tool sold today combines both functions into a single cordless drill driver, with a clutch that lets you switch between controlled screw-driving and full-power drilling. When people ask whether they need a drill or a driver, the honest answer is almost always that they need one drill/driver that does both, which is why every general-purpose pick on this list is described that way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hammer Drill: Built for Masonry</h3>
          <p>
            A hammer drill looks nearly identical to a standard drill/driver, but it adds a mechanism that layers a rapid forward-and-back hammering action on top of the normal rotation, measured in blows per minute (BPM). That hammering action is what actually lets a masonry bit chip its way through brick, concrete block, or poured concrete efficiently. Most hammer drills can switch the hammer function off and work as a completely normal drill/driver, so buying one doesn't mean sacrificing general-purpose use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Impact Driver: Built for High-Torque Fastening</h3>
          <p>
            An impact driver looks similar from a distance but works differently: instead of a hammering motion along the bit's axis, it delivers short, powerful rotational impacts, which is what allows a relatively compact tool to generate torque figures of 1,500 to 2,000-plus in-lbs — two to three times what a standard drill/driver produces. That extra torque makes short work of long deck screws, structural lag bolts, and stuck lug nuts, but the concussive rotational action makes an impact driver a poor choice for precision drilling or delicate driving, where a standard drill/driver's smoother, adjustable-clutch control is the better tool. This is exactly why a drill and impact driver combo kit, like our combo pick below, is such a common professional and DIY purchase — the two tools cover almost every fastening and drilling scenario between them.
          </p>

