          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Battery Platforms: 12V vs 18V/20V vs Higher-Voltage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choosing a cordless drill is really choosing a battery ecosystem, because the batteries and charger will typically outlast several generations of tools if you stay within one platform. Understanding what each voltage tier is actually built for will save you from either overbuying or underbuying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12V Compact Platforms</h3>
          <p>
            Compact 12V systems, like Milwaukee's M12 line, trade raw torque for size and weight. They excel in tight spaces — electrical panels, cabinets, between wall studs — where a full-size drill simply cannot fit or maneuver. A good 12V brushless drill can still deliver 300 to 400 in-lbs of torque, plenty for driving screws, drilling pilot holes, and light general work, but it will bog down on heavy boring or large fasteners in a way a full-size 18V drill won't.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">18V / 20V Standard Platforms</h3>
          <p>
            This is the mainstream tier where the vast majority of drills, including every full-size drill/driver on this list, live. DeWalt brands its version 20V MAX (20V is the peak voltage; the nominal, sustained voltage is 18V, matching Milwaukee, Makita, Ryobi, and Bosch's 18V naming — it's the same tier marketed slightly differently). An 18V or 20V drill/driver comfortably handles general construction, remodeling, furniture assembly, and most homeowner projects, with torque typically ranging from 500 to over 1,200 in-lbs depending on the brand and tier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Higher-Voltage and High-Output Options</h3>
          <p>
            Some brands offer higher-voltage or high-output battery packs designed for demanding tools — DeWalt's FLEXVOLT 60V packs and Milwaukee's HIGH OUTPUT 18V batteries are the best-known examples. These packs are aimed primarily at high-draw tools like circular saws, table saws, and grinders rather than drills specifically, but a high-output battery will noticeably extend runtime and sustained power on a drill during heavy back-to-back use, particularly with a hammer drill drilling repeated masonry anchors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Staying Within One Battery Ecosystem Matters</h3>
          <p>
            Once you own three or four batteries and a charger from one brand, every additional tool you buy within that platform gets cheaper, since bare tools without a battery typically cost 30 to 50 percent less than a full kit. Switching platforms later means starting the battery investment over from zero, which is real money — often $200 to $400 across a modest collection of tools. This is exactly the decision our full Milwaukee vs DeWalt platform comparison walks through in more depth if you're choosing an entire tool ecosystem rather than a single drill, and our Ryobi tools review covers the same trade-offs for the budget-focused ONE+ platform.
          </p>
        </div>

