        <div id="what-to-look-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What to Look For When Buying a Cordless Drill
          </h2>

          <p>
            Spec sheets throw a lot of numbers at buyers, and most of them matter less than four specific factors. Here's what actually determines whether a cordless drill driver will serve you well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Torque Settings and Clutch</h3>
          <p>
            Torque, measured in inch-pounds (in-lbs), is the twisting force the drill can generate — higher figures mean more power for boring large holes or driving big fasteners, but raw torque isn't the whole story. The clutch is what actually protects your work: an adjustable clutch lets the drill slip once a screw reaches a set resistance, preventing stripped screw heads in drywall or blown-through countersinks in trim. A drill with a wide range of clutch settings (15 to 20-plus positions is common on quality tools) gives far more control than one with only a handful of coarse steps, and that control matters more day-to-day than an extra 100 in-lbs of peak torque most projects never actually need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chuck Size and Type</h3>
          <p>
            Most cordless drills use a 1/2-inch keyless chuck, which accepts a wider range of bit shank sizes than the smaller 3/8-inch chucks found on some compact and older models. A metal, single or dual-sleeve chuck that tightens firmly and holds under vibration is worth paying more for — a loose or slipping chuck, a known weak point on some budget drills, wastes time and chews up bit shanks over repeated use. Impact drivers use an entirely different quick-release 1/4-inch hex chuck rather than a keyless design, allowing tool-free bit swaps in seconds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brushless vs Brushed Motors</h3>
          <p>
            Brushless motors have no physical brushes making contact with a spinning commutator, which means less friction, less heat, and dramatically less internal wear over the tool's lifespan. In practical terms, a brushless cordless drill delivers more torque and runtime from the same battery, runs cooler under sustained load, and simply lasts longer than a brushed equivalent. Every drill on this list uses a brushless motor, and at this point we would steer almost any buyer away from a brushed drill driver except at the absolute bottom of the budget tier — the price gap has narrowed enough that brushless is worth paying for in nearly every case.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Battery Included or Bare Tool</h3>
          <p>
            Pay close attention to whether a listed price includes a battery and charger or not. Professional-tier brands like Milwaukee frequently sell drills as a bare tool, assuming buyers already own compatible batteries from other tools in the same platform, while brands like DeWalt and Ryobi more often bundle a kit with batteries included for buyers building a collection from scratch. Bare tools are usually cheaper up front but only save money if you're already invested in that brand's battery ecosystem — otherwise, factor in an additional $80 to $150 for a battery and charger before comparing prices across brands.
          </p>

