        <!-- SECTION 6: Masonry Safety -->
        <div id="masonry-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Safety Notes for Hammer Drilling Into Masonry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drilling into brick, block, or concrete is a fundamentally different — and riskier — task than drilling into wood or drywall, and it deserves a few dedicated precautions that most drill buying guides skip entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Always wear eye protection.</strong> Masonry drilling throws off sharp mineral dust and chips at high velocity, and even a brief, glancing hit can cause serious eye injury. Safety glasses are non-negotiable for this task, no matter how small the hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear a proper dust mask or respirator.</strong> Drilling into concrete and masonry generates fine silica dust, which is a genuine long-term respiratory hazard with repeated exposure. A basic dust mask is the minimum; for extended sessions, a proper N95 or better respirator is worth the extra step, and working outdoors or with ventilation reduces buildup further.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use the correct masonry bit, not a standard wood or metal bit.</strong> Masonry bits use a carbide tip specifically designed to survive the abrasive, high-impact conditions of hammer drilling — a standard twist bit will dull almost immediately and can shatter under hammer action. Match the bit diameter precisely to the anchor or fastener manufacturer's specification; an oversized hole won't hold an anchor securely, and an undersized hole risks cracking the surrounding masonry as you force it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let the tool do the work — don't lean into it.</strong> A hammer drill's concussive action is what breaks up the masonry; excessive forward pressure from the operator doesn't meaningfully speed up drilling and instead increases fatigue, heat buildup, and the risk of the bit binding or wandering off-mark. Keep a firm, steady grip and let the hammer mechanism and bit do the actual cutting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check for hidden electrical, plumbing, or gas lines before drilling.</strong> This applies to any drilling into a wall, but it's especially critical with masonry, where a stud finder with wire and pipe detection (or a look at the structure's as-built plans) can prevent a genuinely dangerous mistake. Never assume a masonry wall is free of embedded conduit or plumbing simply because it's exterior or below grade.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Manage heat and dust buildup on longer jobs.</strong> Repeated masonry holes generate significant heat in both the bit and the tool; pausing periodically to clear dust from the hole and let the bit cool extends bit life and keeps the hammer drill's motor within safe operating temperature, particularly relevant if you're using a standard rather than high-output battery for an extended anchor-setting session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes When Buying a Cordless Drill
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying a cordless drill looks simple, but a handful of predictable mistakes account for most of the buyer's remorse we hear about.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Standard Drill When You Actually Needed a Hammer Drill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If your project involves any brick, block, or concrete anchoring — even occasionally — a standard drill/driver will struggle badly and can overheat trying to force its way through masonry it wasn't designed for. Check whether your project list includes masonry before buying, since retrofitting a masonry job onto the wrong tool wastes time and can damage the drill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Battery Platform You're Buying Into</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A drill is rarely the last cordless tool you'll buy. Choosing a brand based solely on this one purchase, without considering whether you'll eventually want an impact driver, saw, or other tool on the same platform, often means paying for a second battery ecosystem down the line. Think about the next two or three tools you're likely to add before committing to a brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Bare-Tool Prices to Full Kit Prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $99 bare-tool drill and a $149 kit with two batteries and a charger are not comparable prices — factor in what a battery and charger actually cost before assuming one brand is cheaper than another. This is one of the most common ways buyers end up surprised by the real cost of a new tool platform.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overbuying Torque for Light-Duty Homeowner Use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Not every buyer needs a 1,200 in-lbs professional-tier drill. If your projects are mostly furniture assembly, shelving, and the occasional drywall anchor, a lighter, more affordable drill in the 400 to 600 in-lbs range will be plenty, and it will likely be lighter and easier to handle for occasional use than an overbuilt professional model.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cordless drill overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most buyers who want the strongest all-around brushless cordless drill, the Milwaukee M18 FUEL Drill/Driver is our top pick for its torque, build quality, and battery ecosystem. The DeWalt 20V MAX XR is the best-balanced alternative for anyone who wants a lighter, kit-priced option, and the Ryobi ONE+ HP is the best budget cordless drill for homeowners who do not need professional-grade durability.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a drill/driver, a hammer drill, and an impact driver?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A drill/driver uses continuous rotation to bore holes and drive screws, and is the right choice for the vast majority of general tasks. A hammer drill adds a rapid forward hammering action to the rotation, letting it bore into brick, block, and concrete. An impact driver adds rotational hammering blows that deliver much higher torque for driving long screws, lag bolts, and lug nuts, but is not designed for precision drilling. Most homeowners need a drill/driver first, an impact driver second, and a hammer drill only if masonry work is a regular task.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an 18V drill, or is a 12V drill enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12V drill is genuinely capable for light-duty tasks like driving screws, drilling pilot holes, and working in tight spaces such as cabinets or electrical panels, and its smaller size reduces fatigue. An 18V or 20V drill delivers meaningfully more torque and is the better choice for heavier boring, larger fasteners, and general household and light construction use. Many tradespeople carry both, using the 12V as a compact daily driver and the 18V/20V drill for demanding tasks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a brushless cordless drill worth the extra cost over a brushed one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for almost every buyer. Brushless motors run cooler, last significantly longer since there are no brushes to wear out, deliver more torque and runtime per battery charge, and are now standard on every reputable mid-tier and professional drill. The price premium over a brushed drill has narrowed considerably, and the extra cost is worth paying in nearly every case.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I drill into concrete or brick with a regular cordless drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard drill/driver can sometimes bore a small hole into softer masonry with a masonry bit, but it is slow, generates excess heat, and puts unnecessary strain on the motor and chuck. For anything beyond an occasional small anchor hole, a dedicated hammer drill is the right tool — it drills through brick, block, and concrete significantly faster and with far less wear on both the tool and the operator.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much torque do I actually need in a cordless drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For typical homeowner tasks like driving screws, assembling furniture, and drilling holes in wood or drywall, 300 to 500 in-lbs of torque is plenty. For heavier work such as boring large-diameter holes, driving long deck screws, or working with hardwood and engineered lumber, look for 800 in-lbs or more, and pair the drill with an impact driver combo for the highest-torque fastening jobs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

